# Bidirectional file clipboard

Status: initial bidirectional implementation complete behind the explicit
`file_clipboard` Host policy; paired desktop qualification remains pending.

Branch: `bde/file-clipboard-design`. Baseline: BDE root `36cf6aa`, Client
`a954b688`, Linux Host `9acec8c9`, Kymux `912ece5`.

## Implemented slice

The branch now includes text clipboard v1, feature/policy negotiation, separate
bounded control and content lanes, portable v2 framing, Mac-to-X11 staging and
paste release, and X11-to-Mac eager-cache staging with AppKit file-URL
publication. It has no fixed file bitrate ceiling; content uses all available
capacity while yielding to interactive/media pressure.

The Mac receive path deliberately uses the documented eager-cache fallback,
not unqualified `NSFilePromiseProvider` behavior. Transfer progress/cancel UI,
automatic 24-hour cache cleanup, downloaded-file quarantine, concurrent
opposite-direction offers, and paired Finder/Nautilus release qualification
remain follow-up gates. The secure default is therefore still `off`.

## Outcome

A user can copy one or more regular files or directories on a local Mac and
paste them into the connected Linux desktop, or copy them on the Linux desktop
and paste them into a local Mac application such as Finder. The transfer:

- belongs to the authenticated desktop session and user;
- does not pass file contents through the broker or an HTTP control request;
- cannot block keyboard, mouse, audio, video or small text clipboard updates;
- reports preparation, progress, readiness, cancellation and failure;
- never exposes a partially written file as complete;
- preserves names, directory layout, bytes, modification times and portable
  user mode bits, but not ownership, ACLs or extended attributes;
- always copies across machines. It never propagates cut/delete semantics.

The first shipping slice is Mac Client <-> Rocky Linux X11 Host. The wire and
platform boundaries deliberately leave room for the native Mac Host and a
future Windows Client without putting platform clipboard formats on the wire.

## Required baseline integration

The BDE branches do not yet contain PLANK's accepted text clipboard work. File
clipboard must not grow a second ownership, negotiation or lifecycle system.
Before implementation, port the reviewed text clipboard v1 changes onto the BDE
root, Client and Linux Host branches and rerun all broker, remembered-session,
device-session and package gates.

The accepted upstream checkpoint is rooted at `a949440`; its tested package
source is `16c5b998`, with Client `6663d569`, Linux Host `b8abf72c`, Client
common-C `ef8ac14c` and Host common-C `3a97a58f`. Port the focused clipboard
changes rather than replacing the BDE forks with unrelated upstream history.
File clipboard v2 depends on the v1 session epoch, focus policy, pasteboard
ownership checks, X11 worker isolation and 512 KiB text bounds remaining intact.

## Product behavior

### Local Mac to remote Linux

1. Finder or another local application places `NSPasteboardTypeFileURL` items
   on the general pasteboard.
2. Plank notices the file flavor while the stream owns input focus, but sends
   neither metadata nor bytes merely because the user copied something.
3. When the user presses the normal remote paste shortcut, the Client consumes
   that key chord instead of forwarding it immediately. It sends a file offer,
   displays transfer progress and keeps the paste pending.
4. The Host validates policy, names, manifest limits and free space, then pulls
   bounded ranges over the file endpoint into a private session staging area.
5. Each file is written under a temporary name and atomically renamed only
   after its declared length and SHA-256 digest match. After the whole transfer
   is ready, the Host owns X11 `CLIPBOARD` with both `text/uri-list` and
   `x-special/gnome-copied-files` copy representations pointing at the staged
   top-level items.
6. Only after the Host acknowledges that ownership does the Client inject one
   paste chord. Apps that support file clipboard paste then copy the staged
   items into their chosen destination.

If preparation fails or the user cancels, Plank does not forward the withheld
paste. It retains the user's original local clipboard and shows the reason.
Plain-text paste continues through v1 without entering this state machine.

### Remote Linux to local Mac

1. The Host observes a new X11 `CLIPBOARD` owner. It queries `TARGETS`, prefers
   `x-special/gnome-copied-files`, and falls back to `text/uri-list`.
2. It accepts only local `file:` URLs with an empty or local host component.
   A remote `cut` offer is represented as copy across the PLANK boundary.
3. The Host creates a bounded manifest and sends an offer. It does not push file
   content until the Client accepts or requests it.
4. The Client represents each top-level item as an AppKit file promise if the
   native qualification below proves that Finder and supported applications
   consume promises from the general copy/paste pasteboard. A paste then causes
   the promise worker to pull file ranges directly into the destination supplied
   by AppKit.
5. If general-pasteboard file promises are not reliable on every supported
   macOS target, the supported fallback downloads into
   `~/Library/Caches/PLANK/FileClipboard/<offer-id>/`, publishes real file
   URLs only after the complete transfer is ready, and shows a `Ready to paste`
   notification. The previous local clipboard remains untouched while staging.

The fallback is correct but duplicates data when Finder pastes it elsewhere.
It is not the desired large-media path. Do not claim lazy native paste until the
Finder proof passes on macOS 13 and the current qualified macOS release.

Manifest enumeration and file reads run on bounded file-I/O workers. They never
run on the AppKit main queue, SDL event loop, XCB clipboard worker, capture
thread or transport control loop.

### Clipboard precedence

- A real file-URL flavor wins over text flavors from the same pasteboard
  generation. Plank must not leak Finder's path strings through text sync and
  then also start a file transfer.
- Plain text is never interpreted as a path or URL list.
- File promises received from arbitrary local applications are a later
  capability. The first slice accepts stable local file URLs only.
- Re-copying anything replaces the previous offer. A newer local clipboard is
  never overwritten or cleared by completion or cleanup from an older offer.
- Repeated paste from an unchanged completed offer is supported while the
  source lease and staged data remain valid.

## User controls

File clipboard is a distinct permission from text clipboard. The Host
administrator sets the maximum allowed mode:

- `off`
- `client-to-host`
- `host-to-client`
- `bidirectional`

The Client may choose a narrower per-host setting but cannot widen Host policy.
The secure default remains text-only until file clipboard is explicitly
enabled. LoginWindow, the root worker and unauthenticated discovery never read,
advertise or stage files.

The streaming toolbar gains one transfer popover. It shows item count, total
bytes, direction, throughput, current item, and the states `Preparing`,
`Transferring`, `Ready to paste`, `Cancelled` and `Failed`. It provides Cancel
and, for a completed receive, Reveal. Errors name the affected item in the UI
when safe, but filenames and content never enter ordinary logs.

Large-transfer confirmation is a Client preference, with a default threshold
of 1 GiB. Administrative policy can force confirmation for every transfer.

## Negotiation and transport

File clipboard gets a separate launch capability; it does not overload text
feature `0x800000`. The merge-base registry already uses `0x400000` for desktop
sign-out, so the implemented `ClipboardFilesFeature` is `0x1000000`.
Negotiation returns the effective direction policy, limits and staging support.
A peer that lacks the bit keeps text clipboard behavior and never sees a file
endpoint.

Mac launch schema must be bumped rather than silently changing schema 3. Linux
launch responses expose the same effective policy. The capability is valid only
for an authenticated user-desktop worker and is revoked with that worker.

Bulk data must not use the input lane, Host event lane or the existing native
session-data lane. Add two conditional reliable `DataProtocol` endpoints to the
same authenticated QUIC connection:

- endpoint 8: file control and manifests;
- endpoint 10: bounded file-content responses.

Those IDs are the next server-owned even IDs for both the direct endpoint
manifest and a setup connection after promotion. Registration is conditional
on the negotiated file feature on both peers. Keeping control and content on
different QUIC streams prevents a large content record from blocking cancel,
error or lease messages. It also keeps file work independent from setup and
session-ready messages.

The content sender uses 256 KiB maximum chunks, an 8 MiB maximum outstanding
window and no more than two active files. The receiver drives the transfer with
range requests, providing natural backpressure and supporting an AppKit promise
that asks for one top-level item later. Queue limits are enforced before copying
payload bytes.

QUIC reliability and encryption do not by themselves protect interactive
quality because all streams share congestion control. File content therefore
yields whenever input/control queues are nonempty, media queues cross their
low-water mark, RTT doubles from the session baseline, or the transport reports
new unrecovered FEC symbols. There is no fixed file-transfer bitrate ceiling;
when the session is healthy and interactive queues are clear, the transfer may
use the available connection capacity. If Kymux cannot express stream
priority, application yielding is mandatory and a Kymux change is optional,
not a reason to send files on the existing data stream.

The broker does not parse or store this protocol. Direct and relayed sessions
carry it inside the existing end-to-end authenticated QUIC connection. Both
routes require qualification because sustained traffic exercises different
network and idle-time behavior.

The normative message and state contract is in
`protocol/clipboard-files-v2.md`.

## Filesystem contract

The first version transfers regular files, empty files and directories. It
rejects symbolic links, hard-link preservation, sockets, devices, FIFOs and
other special entries. Hidden files are ordinary entries. A macOS package is a
directory tree and transfers under the same rules.

The manifest contains only relative UTF-8 name components and platform-neutral
metadata. It never sends an absolute source path. Receivers reject:

- empty names, `.`, `..`, `/`, NUL or invalid UTF-8;
- path depth over 64;
- components over 255 UTF-8 bytes;
- canonical or case-folded destination collisions;
- more than 1,024 top-level items or 100,000 total entries;
- manifests over 32 MiB;
- a total declared size over the effective policy cap;
- any integer overflow in counts, sizes or offsets.

The initial policy cap is 1 TiB per transfer and two transfers total, one per
direction. Both are administrator-reducible. The receiver also checks current
free space and its private staging quota. `ENOSPC` remains a handled transfer
failure because a preflight cannot reserve all filesystem capacity.

Destination files belong to the authenticated desktop user. Plank creates
private directories as `0700` and partial files as `0600`, applies the user's
umask, preserves portable user read/write/execute bits and directory execute
bits, and strips setuid, setgid and sticky bits. It preserves nanosecond mtime
when available. It does not transfer uid/gid, ACLs, resource forks, Finder
metadata, Linux capabilities or arbitrary extended attributes in v2.

The Host staging root is an administrator-configurable directory on a real
disk, defaulting to the authenticated user's XDG cache, never `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR`
or another likely tmpfs. The Mac fallback uses the user cache path above.
Partial trees use a non-addressable `.partial` name and become clipboard-visible
through one atomic directory rename.

Completed staged content stays available while Plank still owns the related
clipboard, plus a bounded grace period so the destination can finish reading.
Unowned entries expire after 24 hours and are removed on the next Client/Host
start. Cleanup checks the private root, transfer marker and ownership before
deletion; it never recursively deletes a path supplied by the peer.

## Source stability and integrity

Opening a source by path and trusting an earlier manifest is a TOCTOU bug.
Source registries are local and session-scoped:

- Linux opens top-level roots as the desktop user, walks with directory file
  descriptors, uses `openat2` with `RESOLVE_BENEATH | RESOLVE_NO_MAGICLINKS`
  where available, and rejects symlinks. The fallback uses `openat`,
  `O_NOFOLLOW` and an explicit component walk.
- macOS accepts only local file URLs, opens without following symlinks, and
  validates the opened object against its recorded device, inode, size, ctime
  and mtime before serving content.
- A sender rechecks stable metadata after the last range. Mutation cancels the
  affected transfer instead of delivering a mixed snapshot.
- Sender and receiver calculate SHA-256 while serving sequential ranges. The
  receiver renames a partial file only after size and final digest match.

QUIC already protects packets in transit; the digest detects source mutation,
implementation errors and incorrect range assembly.

## Session and clipboard lifecycle

Every offer carries a random 128-bit offer ID, clipboard generation and session
epoch. Each paste/materialization adds a nonzero 64-bit materialization ID so a
still-owned clipboard can be pasted more than once without reusing terminal
transfer state. Messages from another epoch are rejected.

- Before a materialization starts, a changed source clipboard sends Revoke and
  closes its source registry. A valid Materialize request locks that exact
  operation. A clipboard change then prevents another materialization but
  does not corrupt or cancel the paste already in progress. This matches normal
  file-copy behavior: copying something new does not abort an active copy.
- A changed destination clipboard cancels an unstarted promise only if Plank
  still owns that exact pasteboard change count or X11 selection. It does not
  abort a materialization already writing to an application's destination.
  Eager fallback staging may finish, but must not overwrite the newer clipboard;
  its popover exposes an explicit `Copy files` action instead.
- Disconnect, logout, desktop handoff, takeover, account change and worker
  replacement cancel active transfers, close source handles and delete partial
  destination data.
- Completed fallback data may remain under the bounded cache policy, but its
  old transfer capability is never valid in a new session.
- File promises fail clearly after disconnect; v2 does not resume across a new
  authenticated connection.
- Cancellation is idempotent. Late chunks are ignored after state is terminal.

## Security properties

- Only the authenticated active desktop owner can negotiate or access files.
- Direction policy is enforced independently at both peers.
- No file metadata or byte is accepted before feature negotiation and policy
  agreement.
- Client-to-Host content begins only after an explicit remote paste gesture.
- X11 offers are accepted only from the active user's `CLIPBOARD`; `PRIMARY`
  does not trigger file transfer.
- URI lists accept local file URLs only. Network URLs and text paths are never
  dereferenced.
- Remote cut becomes copy. No peer can request source deletion.
- Files are never executed, opened or previewed automatically.
- Mac receives should apply ordinary downloaded-file quarantine provenance;
  executable and application-bundle behavior needs security review during the
  native spike.
- Logs include direction, state, counts, byte totals, duration and stable error
  codes, never paths, names, content, tokens or digests.
- Malformed control records terminate the file subsystem and transfer. They do
  not need to tear down video unless endpoint framing or session authentication
  is compromised.

## Implementation phases

### Phase 0: prove platform behavior and port text v1

- Port the accepted text clipboard implementation onto BDE branches.
- Build a throwaway native Mac probe that writes `NSFilePromiseProvider` objects
  to `NSPasteboard.general` and verify ordinary Cmd-V into Finder on macOS 13
  and the current qualified macOS release. Also test multiple files, a directory,
  cancellation and a promise completing after the source app loses focus.
- Capture actual Finder pasteboard types for copied files. Do not infer support
  from drag-and-drop documentation; Apple explicitly distinguishes the dragging
  pasteboard from normal copy/paste.
- On Rocky X11, capture Nautilus and the deployed file manager's `TARGETS` and
  payloads for copy and cut. Prove that publishing both URI formats supports
  paste into the supported file manager.
- Record the chosen Mac strategy in this plan before implementing bulk transfer.

### Phase 1: protocol and transport

- Add the feature allocation, launch-policy response and fixed v2 wire helper.
- Add conditional control/content DataProtocol endpoints, bounded queues,
  priority yielding and metrics to `plank-transport`.
- Add portable encode/decode vectors, malformed-frame tests, queue-pressure
  tests and fuzz/ASan/UBSan coverage before platform code consumes the API.

### Phase 2: Client to Linux Host vertical slice (implemented)

- Read stable Mac file URLs and intercept file paste.
- Build manifests without following links.
- Implement the Linux receiver, private stage, atomic completion and X11 URI
  provider.
- Release the paste chord only after the Host confirms X11 ownership.
- Add progress/cancel UI and policy controls. (Policy is implemented; UI is
  pending.)

### Phase 3: Linux Host to Mac Client vertical slice (implemented with eager fallback)

- Extend the isolated XCB worker to query file targets and parse URI lists with
  INCR support and separate limits from text.
- Implement the Linux source registry and Mac destination writer.
- Use qualified AppKit promises or the explicitly reported eager fallback.
- Preserve newer local pasteboard ownership through cancel and teardown.

### Phase 4: hardening and release qualification (pending)

- Exercise direct and broker-relayed routes under sustained video/audio/input.
- Validate limits, disk-full, source mutation, permission denial, disconnect,
  reconnect, takeover, logout/login and crash cleanup.
- Build branch-qualified Host and Client packages before enabling the default
  or publishing a release. The implementation may merge while default-off so
  paired acceptance can run from the shared repository.

### Phase 5: Mac Host

Reuse the protocol and AppKit adapter inside the authorized desktop worker.
Keep LoginWindow disabled, access NSPasteboard on the main queue, and retain the
existing authority callback and change-count ownership rules. This phase cannot
weaken the Linux Host release gate.

## Verification matrix

Automated coverage must protect behavior, not source spelling:

- single file, empty file, multiple files and nested directory round trips;
- Unicode, decomposed Unicode, hidden files, executable user bit and mtime;
- exact-limit names/counts/manifest/total bytes and one-over-limit rejection;
- invalid UTF-8, traversal, absolute names, duplicate/case-folded names,
  symlink, FIFO, device and integer-overflow rejection;
- peer sends data before Ack, wrong epoch/transfer/entry, overlapping or
  out-of-order range, overlong chunk, wrong size/digest and late data;
- source changes during transfer, unreadable source and destination `ENOSPC`;
- cancel at offer, manifest, content and atomic-rename boundaries;
- copy A -> B -> A, re-copy identical items, newer local clipboard, repeated
  paste and paste immediately after copy;
- disconnect/reconnect, takeover, desktop handoff, login/logout and process
  crash with no reusable capability or surviving partial file;
- no file endpoints or filesystem access when policy is text-only;
- direct and relayed 20 GiB transfer while video, audio, keyboard, mouse and
  Wacom remain usable, with no control queue overflow and no unbounded memory;
- SHA-256 equality for every received file and deterministic tree comparison.

Manual acceptance must cover Finder and the supported Rocky file manager in
both directions, a directory with thousands of media files, a file larger than
4 GiB, repeated paste, transfer cancellation, insufficient disk, Spaces/focus
changes, and a destination application that does not accept file clipboard
formats. That last case must fail visibly; Plank must not claim that every
remote application implements file paste.

## External protocol references

- Apple's `NSFilePromiseProvider` contract:
  <https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appkit/nsfilepromiseprovider>
- Apple's warning that dragging uses a pasteboard distinct from normal
  copy/paste:
  <https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appkit/supporting-drag-and-drop-through-file-promises>
- Microsoft's mature pull/range model for remote clipboard files:
  <https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/openspecs/windows_protocols/ms-rdpeclip/30688d09-96b6-46f8-af18-ea1998bb7987>
- GNOME's file clipboard representations:
  <https://mail.gnome.org/archives/commits-list/2022-January/msg01597.html>
- Freedesktop clipboard specifications:
  <https://specifications.freedesktop.org/>
